To create a debouncing function in JavaScript, you can use the following approach. Debouncing means delaying the execution of a function until a certain amount of time has passed without the function being called again. Here's a simple debouncing function:

```javascript
function debounce(func, wait) {
  let timeout;

  return function () {
    const context = this;
    const args = arguments;

    clearTimeout(timeout);
    timeout = setTimeout(function () {
      func.apply(context, args);
    }, wait);
  };
}
```

You can use this debouncing function to debounce another function, for example:

```javascript
const debouncedFunction = debounce(function () {
  console.log('Debounced function called');
}, 300);

// Call the debounced function multiple times within 300ms, but it will only be called once every 300ms
debouncedFunction();
debouncedFunction();
debouncedFunction();
```

In this example, the debounced function will be called only once every 300ms, even if you call it multiple times within that period.

